  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The correct method in this scenario is to <b>build an Amazon Machine Image (AMI) from the EC2 instance before copying it to the new region</b>. This process preserves the configuration and all data on the instance, and the copied AMI can then be launched in any supported AWS region, ensuring a consistent and reliable replica.<br><br>Let's review the other options:</p><ul><li><i>Just take an AMI of the EC2 instance only</i>: While creating an AMI is a valid start, AMIs are region-specific. If you only create an AMI without copying it to the destination region, you won't be able to launch an instance based on that AMI outside of its original region.</li><li><i>Use IAM permissions to allow sharing of the EC2 instance across regions</i>: EC2 instances themselves cannot be shared across multiple regions. IAM permissions let you control access to AWS resources, but do not enable instances to be directly used in another region.</li><li><i>None of these choices are suitable</i>: There is actually a correct choice among the options, so this answer is incorrect.</li></ul><br><b>Tip:</b> Always remember, AMIs are region-bound. To use the same image in another region, you must explicitly copy it to the target region.<br><br>This method ensures your disaster recovery environment is ready to launch with identical settings and applications as your original deployment.",
  "query": "<p>You have been assigned to set up a replicated environment in a different AWS region for disaster recovery. Some components of your solution depend on EC2 instances that have specific software installed and configured.<br>What should you do to properly reproduce these EC2 instances in the new region? Select the best answer from the choices below.</p>",
